# Function Declarations & Hoisting 📢

A function declaration defines a named function using the `function` keyword:

```javascript
// Invoking before declaration works because of hoisting!
greet("Alice");

function greet(name) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
greet("Alice");

function greet(name) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}
FULL_CODE_END -->

## Hoisting Explained:
During the compilation phase before code runs, JavaScript moves function declarations to the top of their containing scope. You can safely call them before their line of definition!

# Function Expressions 📦

A function expression assigns an anonymous (or named) function to a variable:

```javascript
// Function expressions are NOT hoisted!
const calculateTax = function(price, rate = 0.08) {
  return price * rate;
};

const tax = calculateTax(100);
console.log("Calculated tax:", tax);
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const calculateTax = function(price, rate = 0.08) {
  return price * rate;
};

const tax = calculateTax(100);
console.log("Calculated tax:", tax);
FULL_CODE_END -->

## First-Class Citizens:
In JavaScript, functions are first-class citizens. They can be stored in variables, passed as arguments to other functions, and returned from functions!

# Return Values & Early Exit ↩️

Functions return a value using the `return` statement. If no `return` is provided, the function implicitly returns `undefined`:

```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) {
    return "Error: Division by zero!"; // Early exit
  }
  return a / b;
}

console.log(divide(10, 2));
console.log(divide(10, 0));
```

