# Returning Objects from Arrow Functions 📦

When using implicit return with an object literal, wrap the object in parentheses `({ ... })` so the parser doesn't mistake curly braces for a function block:

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG: Syntax error or returns undefined
// const makeUser = (id, name) => { id: id, name: name };

// ✅ CORRECT: Wrap object in parentheses
const makeUser = (id, name) => ({ id: id, name: name });

console.log(makeUser(1, "David"));
```

<!-- FULL_CODE_START
const makeUser = (id, name) => ({ id: id, name: name });

console.log("Created user:", makeUser(1, "David"));
FULL_CODE_END -->

# Lexical `this` in Arrow Functions 🧭

The most important architectural difference between regular functions and arrow functions:

- **Regular Functions:** Define their own `this` dynamically depending on how they are called.
- **Arrow Functions:** Do **not** have their own `this`! They lexically inherit `this` from the enclosing parent scope.

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Jessica",
  hobbies: ["Coding", "Hiking"],
  printHobbies() {
    // Arrow function preserves outer 'this' pointing to user!
    this.hobbies.forEach(hobby => {
      console.log(`${this.name} loves ${hobby}`);
    });
  }
};

user.printHobbies();
```

